      "solution": "Shortcut: Use King's Property ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ f(x)dx = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ f(a-x)dx. Let I be the integral. Replacing x by π/2 - x gives I = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ (cos x)/(sin x + cos x) dx. Adding both forms: 2I = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ 1 dx = π/2 ⇒ I = π/4. Full Solution: Let I = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ sin x/(sin x+cos x) dx. Substitute x → π/2 - x, using sin(π/2-x)=cos x, cos(π/2-x)=sin x. I = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ cos x/(sin x+cos x) dx. Add the two equations: 2I = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ (sin x+cos x)/(sin x+cos x) dx = ∫₀⁰ⁿ₂ dx = [x]₀⁰ⁿ₂π/2 = π/2. Hence I = π/4.",
